  "story": {
    "title": "AI Agents Will Change Software Development",
    "summary": "AI agents are shifting software development from manually executing every task toward directing systems that plan, build, test and improve work under human supervision.",
    "hook": "The biggest change in software development will not be better autocomplete.",
    "openLoop": "The real shift happens when AI stops waiting for every instruction.",
    "payoff": "Developers will increasingly direct, review and govern AI-driven workflows instead of manually executing every step.",
    "callToAction": "Learn to direct the systems, not just use the tools.",
    "fullNarration": "The biggest change in software development will not be better autocomplete. Today, most AI tools wait for you to ask a question. AI agents work differently. Give an agent a goal and it can break that goal into tasks, inspect files, write code, run tests and correct failures. That does not remove developers from the process. It changes where developers create value. Less time may go into repetitive execution, while more time goes into architecture, judgment, security and validation. The developer of the future may not write every line manually. But they will be responsible for directing the system, reviewing its decisions and proving that the result is safe. Learn to direct the systems, not just use the tools.",
    "gateScore": 80,
    "followUps": [
      "What can an AI agent still not do?",
      "How does an AI agent test its own code?",
      "Will AI agents need managers?"
    ]
  },
